    4|       |/**
    5|       | *
    6|       | * This file contains a set of functions used by multiple procedures as
    7|       | * internal functions. These procedures should not be accessed directly by a
    8|       | * user. Note that all procedures are either macros or static inline procedures.
    9|       | *
   10|       | * Synopsis
   11|       | * ========
   12|       | *
   13|       | * .. code-block:: c
   14|       | *
   15|       | *     #include "tools.h"
   16|       | *     EXTRACT_WORDS(ix0,ix1,d);                // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, int, double)
   17|       | *     GET_HIGH_WORD(i,d);                      // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, double)
   18|       | *     GET_LOW_WORD(i,d);                       // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, double)
   19|       | *     INSERT_WORDS(d,ix0,ix1);                 // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int, int)
   20|       | *     SET_HIGH_WORD(d,v);                      // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int)
   21|       | *     SET_LOW_WORD(d,v);                       // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (double, int)
   22|       | *     GET_FLOAT_WORD(i,f);                     // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (int, float)
   23|       | *     SET_FLOAT_WORD(f,i);                     // this macro has no return value, the inputs are expected to be (float, int)
   24|       | *     SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT(op,amt);                // this macros return value has the same type as input `op`, the inputs are expected to be integer types
   25|       | *     double __forced_calculation(double x);
   26|       | *     float __forced_calculationf(float x);
   27|       | *     double __raise_invalid(void);
   28|       | *     float __raise_invalidf(void);
   29|       | *     double __raise_div_by_zero(double x);
   30|       | *     float __raise_div_by_zerof(float x);
   31|       | *     double __raise_overflow(double x);
   32|       | *     float __raise_overflowf(float x);
   33|       | *     double __raise_underflow(double x);
   34|       | *     float __raise_underflowf(float x);
   35|       | *     double __raise_inexact(double x);
   36|       | *     float __raise_inexactf(float x);
   37|       | *     int __issignaling(double x);
   38|       | *     int __issignalingf(float x);
   39|       | *     REAL_PART(z);                            // this macros return value has the non-complex type of input `z`, the input is expected to be a complex floating-point datum
   40|       | *     IMAG_PART(z);                            // this macros return value has the non-complex type of input `z`, the input is expected to be a complex floating-point datum
   41|       | *
   42|       | * Description
   43|       | * ===========
   44|       | *
   45|       | * ``EXTRACT_WORDS`` is a macro to extract two integer words from a double
   46|       | * floating-point datum.
   47|       | *
   48|       | * ``GET_HIGH_WORD`` is a macro to extract only the highword of the two integer
   49|       | * words from a double floating-point datum.
   50|       | *
   51|       | * ``GET_LOW_WORD`` is a macro to extract only the lowword of the two integer
   52|       | * words from a double floating-point datum.
   53|       | *
   54|       | * ``INSERT_WORDS`` is a macro to insert two integer words into a double
   55|       | * floating-point datum.
   56|       | *
   57|       | * ``SET_HIGH_WORD`` is a macro to insert only the highword of the two integer
   58|       | * words into a double floating-point datum.
   59|       | *
   60|       | * ``SET_LOW_WORD`` is a macro to insert only the lowword of the two integer
   61|       | * words into a double floating-point datum.
   62|       | *
   63|       | * ``GET_FLOAT_WORD`` is a macro to extract the integer representation from a
   64|       | * single floating-point datum.
   65|       | *
   66|       | * ``SET_FLOAT_WORD`` is a macro to insert the integer representation into a
   67|       | * single floating-point datum.
   68|       | *
   69|       | * ``SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT`` is a macro to avoid undefined behaviour that can arise
   70|       | * if the amount of a right shift is exactly equal to the size of the shifted
   71|       | * operand. If the amount is equal to the size the macro returns 0.
   72|       | *
   73|       | * ``__forced_calculation`` is a function to force the execution of the input
   74|       | * to go through the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>`. The input for this function is usually
   75|       | * an arithmetic operation and not a single value. At the moment the function
   76|       | * is only used by others within this file and not expected to be called from
   77|       | * outside.
   78|       | *
   79|       | * ``__raise_invalid`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to generate
   80|       | * an ``invalid operation`` exception.
   81|       | *
   82|       | * ``__raise_div_by_zero`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to
   83|       | * generate an ``divide by zero`` exception.
   84|       | *
   85|       | * ``__raise_overflow`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to
   86|       | * generate an ``overflow`` exception.
   87|       | *
   88|       | * ``__raise_underflow`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to
   89|       | * generate an ``underflow`` exception. Even though the library usually does
   90|       | * not care about this exception for qualification purposes, the library still
   91|       | * tries to conform to the standards (mainly POSIX) in regards to where the
   92|       | * exception shall be raised intentionally. In those places this function is
   93|       | * used.
   94|       | *
   95|       | * ``__raise_inexact`` is a function to force the :ref:`FPU <ABBR>` to generate
   96|       | * an ``inexact`` exception. Even though the library usually does not care
   97|       | * about this exception for qualification purposes, the library still tries to
   98|       | * conform to the standards (mainly POSIX) in regards to where the exception
   99|       | * shall be raised intentionally. In those places this function is used.
  100|       | *
  101|       | * ``__issignaling`` is a function to check if a value is a signaling NaN.
  102|       | *
  103|       | * ``REAL_PART`` is a macro to extract only the real part of a complex
  104|       | * floating-point datum.
  105|       | *
  106|       | * ``IMAG_PART`` is a macro to extract only the imaginary part of a complex
  107|       | * floating-point datum.
  108|       | *
  109|       | * Mathematical Function
  110|       | * =====================
  111|       | *
  112|       | * .. math::
  113|       | *
  114|       | *    EXTRACT\_WORDS_{ix0}(d) &= \text{highword of } d  \\
  115|       | *    EXTRACT\_WORDS_{ix1}(d) &= \text{lowword of } d  \\
  116|       | *    GET\_HIGH\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \text{highword of } d  \\
  117|       | *    GET\_LOW\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \text{lowword of } d  \\
  118|       | *    SET\_HIGH\_WORD_{\text{highword of } d}(d, v) &= d \text{ with highword } v  \\
  119|       | *    SET\_LOW\_WORD_{\text{lowword of } d}(d, v) &= d \text{ with lowword } v  \\
  120|       | *    GET\_FLOAT\_WORD_{i}(d) &= \text{integer representation of } d  \\
  121|       | *    SET\_FLOAT\_WORD_{d}(i) &= \text{floating-point representation of } i  \\
  122|       | *    SAFE\_RIGHT\_SHIFT(op, amt) &= \left\{\begin{array}{ll} op \gg amt, & amt < \text{size of } op \\ 0, & otherwise \end{array}\right.  \\
  123|       | *    \_\_forced\_calculation(x) &= x  \\
  124|       | *    \_\_raise\_invalid() &= NaN  \\
  125|       | *    \_\_raise\_div\_by\_zero(x) &= \left\{\begin{array}{ll} -Inf, & x \in \mathbb{F}^{-} \\ +Inf, & otherwise \end{array}\right.  \\
  126|       | *    \_\_raise\_overflow(x) &= \left\{\begin{array}{ll} -Inf, & x \in \mathbb{F}^{-} \\ +Inf, & otherwise \end{array}\right.  \\
  127|       | *    \_\_raise\_underflow(x) &= \left\{\begin{array}{ll} -0.0, & x \in \mathbb{F}^{-} \\ +0.0, & otherwise \end{array}\right.  \\
  128|       | *    \_\_raise\_inexact(x) &= x  \\
  129|       | *    \_\_issignaling(x) &= \left\{\begin{array}{ll} 1, & x = sNaN \\ 0, & otherwise \end{array}\right.  \\
  130|       | *    REAL\_PART(z) &= \Re(z)  \\
  131|       | *    IMAG\_PART(z) &= \Im(z)
  132|       | *
  133|       | * Returns
  134|       | * =======
  135|       | *
  136|       | * ``EXTRACT_WORDS`` has no return value. It places the two extracted integer
  137|       | * words into the parameters ``ix0`` and ``ix1``.
  138|       | *
  139|       | * ``GET_HIGH_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word
  140|       | * into the parameter ``i``.
  141|       | *
  142|       | * ``GET_LOW_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word
  143|       | * into the parameter ``i``.
  144|       | *
  145|       | * ``INSERT_WORDS`` has no return value. It places the created double
  146|       | * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.
  147|       | *
  148|       | * ``SET_HIGH_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created double
  149|       | * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.
  150|       | *
  151|       | * ``SET_LOW_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created double
  152|       | * floating-point datum into the parameter ``d``.
  153|       | *
  154|       | * ``GET_FLOAT_WORD`` has no return value. It places the extracted integer word
  155|       | * into the parameter ``i``.
  156|       | *
  157|       | * ``SET_FLOAT_WORD`` has no return value. It places the created single
  158|       | * floating-point datum into the parameter ``f``.
  159|       | *
  160|       | * ``SAFE_RIGHT_SHIFT`` returns the value ``op`` right shifted by ``amt`` if
  161|       | * ``amt`` is smaller than the size of ``op``, otherwise it returns zero.
  162|       | *
  163|       | * ``__forced_calculation`` returns the input value, or rather the result of
  164|       | * the arithmetic calculation used as an input.
  165|       | *
  166|       | * ``__raise_invalid`` returns ``NaN``.
  167|       | *
  168|       | * ``__raise_div_by_zero`` returns ``-Inf`` for negative inputs and ``+Inf``
  169|       | * for positive inputs.
  170|       | *
  171|       | * ``__raise_overflow`` returns ``-Inf`` for negative inputs and ``+Inf`` for
  172|       | * positive inputs.
  173|       | *
  174|       | * ``__raise_underflow`` returns ``-0.0`` for negative inputs and ``+0.0`` for
  175|       | * positive inputs.
  176|       | *
  177|       | * ``__raise_inexact`` returns the input value.
  178|       | *
  179|       | * ``__issignaling`` returns ``1`` if the input value is a signaling ``NaN``,
  180|       | * else ``0``.
  181|       | *
  182|       | * ``REAL_PART`` returns the real part of the input as a floating-point datum
  183|       | * of the adequate size.
  184|       | *
  185|       | * ``IMAG_PART`` returns the imaginary part of the input as a floating-point
  186|       | * datum of the adequate size.
  187|       | *
  188|       | * Exceptions
  189|       | * ==========
  190|       | *
  191|       | * The macros do not raise exceptions.
  192|       | *
  193|       | * The functions raise the exceptions they are named after, it is their sole
  194|       | * purpose. Additionally the procedures ``__raise_overflow`` and
  195|       | * ``__raise_underflow`` may raise ``inexact``.
  196|       | *
  197|       | */
